data.content:
## What breaks in .NET 11

The **sync-over-async opt-in for the Cosmos DB provider has been removed**. In .NET 10, you could call `EnableSyncOverAsync()` (or the equivalent option) to let synchronous LINQ operators work by blocking on async calls internally. That escape hatch was always marked as **temporary/deprecated**, and .NET 11 removes it entirely.

Your sync calls — `ToList()`, `First()`, `Count()`, and `SaveChanges()` — will now throw **`InvalidOperationException`** at runtime.

### Fix: convert to async

```csharp
public class ProductRepository
{
    private readonly CosmosDbContext _context;

    public async Task<List<Product>> GetAllAsync()
    {
        return await _context.Products.ToListAsync();
    }

    public async Task<Product> GetByIdAsync(int id)
    {
        return await _context.Products.FirstAsync(p => p.Id == id);
    }

    public async Task<int> GetCountAsync()
    {
        return await _context.Products.CountAsync();
    }

    public async Task SaveAsync(Product product)
    {
        _context.Products.Add(product);
        await _context.SaveChangesAsync();
    }
}
```

**Key points:**

- `Add()` stays synchronous — it only tracks the entity in memory; no I/O occurs until `SaveChangesAsync()`.
- All callers up the chain must also become `async`/`await` (the "async all the way up" rule).
- The async LINQ methods (`ToListAsync`, `FirstAsync`, etc.) come from `Microsoft.EntityFrameworkCore` — make sure you have `using Microsoft.EntityFrameworkCore;`.
